## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) [1–4] are a class of porous crystalline materials that have attracted tremendous attentions in last two decades, and have been considered as promising materials for various key applications. The structural richness of MOFs lies in the fact that (i) diversified secondary building units (SBUs) [5] could be designed by stitching several metal ions into rigid entities with metal–linker coordination, and (ii) with the modularity of reticular chemistry [6], a series of MOFs following the same topology but with different functionalities could be systematically achieved. The ability to create a whole library of MOF structures with high surface areas, specific functionalities, and satisfactory chemical and thermal stabilities has endowed the materials with promising applications in gas storage [7] and separation [8,9], and catalysis [10] etc. Among the SBUs employed as the nodes for the MOF construction, the hexanuclear $[\mathsf{M}_{6}(\mathsf{C O O})_{12}]$ node as seen in UiO-66 [11], has shown great interests due to its high number of connectivity and enhanced chemical stability in practical conditions [12–15]. Recently, utilizing the SBU’s high tolerance towards vacancy chemistry [16] and linker installation modification [17,18], multi-functional $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -based MOFs have been reported.
The rare earth elements, such as terbium and europium, have been demonstrated to be able to construct the same 12-connected hexanuclear nodes as zirconium. For example, Eddaoudi, Rosi, and Yuan et al. reported a series of MOFs with truncated face-centered cubic (fcu-a) topology, based on the $\mathrm{[Tb_{6}(O H)_{8}(C O O)_{12}]}$ units and ditopic organic linkers independently [19–23]. On top of the fascinating structural merits from the SBUs and the linkers, these MOFs feature characteristic luminescence properties stemming from the metal’s f-f electron transition. Thus, they have been investigated for the applications in cooperative gas separation [21], near infrared excitation and emission [22], and barcode recognition [23]. However, the examples of rare earth MOFs with fcu-a topology are still very limited, and exercising the reticular chemistry approach will definitely construct new interfaces functionalized by the linkers within the MOFs, and equip the materials with luminescence properties from the metals.
Herein, by selecting the organic linkers judiciously, we synthesized four isostructural MOFs based on $\mathrm{[Tb_{6}(O H)_{8}(C O O)_{12}]}$ , named FDM-34–37 $\mathrm{\FDM=}$ Fudan Materials) in single crystal form successfully. The four dicarboxylic acids with different functionalities employed in the study had the length spanning from 17.4 to $23.8\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ when serving as the edges for the fcu-a networks. All four MOFs exhibited permanent porosities with high surface areas. With the bipyridine functionality on the pore interface, FDM-35 had a surface area of $1850\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and two kinds of distinct micropores were observed. Furthermore, the discriminated luminescence properties of FDM-34–37 were investigated, and their relationship to the different triplet excited state energy of the linkers were described. The presented work enriched the structural diversity of Tb-based MOFs. With characteristic luminescence and potentially active sites from both the metals and the linkers on the interfaces, these MOFs show prospects in sensing and catalysis applications.
