## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Crystal structures and physical characterizations of FDM-34–37
A solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{Tb}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{NDC}}$ in DMF and ethanol afforded single crystals of FDM-34. During the reaction, 2-FBA was added as a modulator and structure-directing agent [21,24]. When $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B P y D C}$ , ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{S B D C}}$ or ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf T P D C-M e}_{2}$ was employed as the organic linker under similar reaction condition, single crystals of FDM-35, FDM-36, and FDM-37 were obtained, respectively. All four structures were crystallized in cubic space groups (Table S1), and isoreticular structures were revealed by SXRD. In all cases, each Tb(III) was coordinated with four bridging $\mu_{3}–0\mathrm{H}$ groups, four carboxylic groups, and one terminal water molecule. Six Tb(III) were joined by $\mu_{3}–0\mathrm{H}$ and $-\mathtt{C O O}^{-}$ to form a $\mathrm{Tb}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}0\mathrm{H})_{8}(\mathrm{COO})_{12}$ cluster (Fig. 1a). This cuboctahedron cluster served as a 12-connected SBU for the MOF construction. Combining the SBU with rigid organic struts (Fig. 1b) has achieved a series of three-dimensional frameworks with fcu-a topology (Fig. 1c). fcu-a MOFs with the same 12-connected Tb-based SBUs were previously reported in the literatures (Table S2) [19–23], and FDM34–37 are also isoreticular with the benchmark Zr-based UiO-66 [11]. Notably, the same structure of FDM-34 with NDC linkers was previously reported by Rosi et al. [22]. In addition, the use of 2-FBA in the synthesis was crucial for the successful crystallization of FDM-34–37. It was proposed that it repelled water molecules and facilitated the formation of this particular SBU during the synthesis [25].
FDM-34 has an edge length of $17.4\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ (by measuring the distance between two neighboring SBU centers), and the lengths increase to 19.3, 21.8, and $23.8\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ for FDM-35–37, respectively. Two kinds of polyhedral cages are located in these structures: the octahedral cage formed by 6 SBUs and 12 linkers, and the tetrahedral cage formed by 4 SBUs and 6 linkers (Fig. 1d and e). Measured from the crystal structure, FDM-34 features octahedral pores with a size of $10.2\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and tetrahedral pores with a size of $\bar{7}.6\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ (diameter of the largest sphere that can fit inside the cage without touching the interior van der Waals surface). On the other hand, with the longer linkers, FDM-35, -36, and -37 have the octahedral pores with sizes of 12.8, 15.8, and $17.6\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , and the tetrahedral pores with sizes of 10.2, 12.0, and $8.0\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , respectively. It was expected that FDM-37 should have the tetrahedral cages with larger voids than the other three MOFs. However, due to the dihedral angle of $67.1^{\circ}$ between the central phenyl ring and the phenyl rings in the ends of the TPDC- $\cdot\ensuremath{\mathrm{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{e}}_{2}$ , the dimethyl groups pointed to the center of the tetrahedral cage and made the pore size smaller. Assuming the central phenyl ring can rotate, the size of the tetrahedral cages in FDM-37 would be $13.6\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ instead.
Structures of all four MOFs were thoroughly characterized, and here we show FDM-35 as an example. XPS was used to confirm the valence of Tb in FDM-35. As shown in Fig. 2a, the peaks at 1240.2 and $1248.2\:\mathrm{eV}$ were assigned to be Tb $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ , along with the $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ peak at $1274.8\mathrm{eV}$ , indicating the $^{+3}$ state of Tb in FDM-35 [26,27]. Thus, the formula of the FDM-35 framework skeleton is $[\mathrm{Tb}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}})_{8}(\mathrm{BPyDC})_{6}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}]^{2-}$ based on the SXRD analysis. Due to the porous nature of the framework, cations inside the pores were disordered and could not be precisely located in the crystal structure. Based on the solution $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR of the modulator 2-FBA (Fig. S4) and the DCl-digested FDM-34–37 (Figs. S5–S8), we not only confirmed the presence of $[(\mathsf{C H}_{3})_{2}\mathsf{N H}_{2}]^{+}$ in the frameworks, but also excluded the possibility of 2-FBA incorporation in the structures. $[(\mathsf{C H}_{3})_{2}\mathsf{N H}_{2}]^{+}$ were formed in situ via the decomposition of DMF solvent during the synthesis [23]. Thus, we concluded the chemical formula of FDM-35 to be $\mathrm{\small{[(CH_{3})_{2}N H_{2}]_{2}[T b_{6}}}$ $(\mu_{3}–0\mathrm{H})_{8}(\mathrm{BPyDC})_{6}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{6}]$ . The MOFs’ phase purities were validated by comparing their PXRD patterns with the ones simulated from crystal structures (Figs. 2b and S1). In addition, all these structures could be heated up to $400^{\circ}\mathsf C$ without obvious weight loss (Fig. S2), suggesting high thermal stability of these MOFs.
## 3.2. Pore interface studies on FDM-34–37
Based on the single crystal structures, all four MOFs are highly porous. For example, including the cations in the framework, FDM-37 has a crystal density of only $0.58\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . What is more, all structures feature two kinds of micropores with distinct pore sizes, providing diversified pore interfaces available to incoming guests. ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms of FDM-34–37 were measured to investigate their surface areas and pore volumes. Before the measurements, all MOFs were activated either by heating under vacuum or by supercritical ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ drying after solvent exchange. These MOFs retained their original structures and crystallinity after activation, as indicated by the coincidence in the PXRD patterns compared with the simulations (Figs. 2b and S1). The isotherms for all four samples showed typical type-I adsorption behavior (Fig. 2c). The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of FDM-34 was calculated to be $1305{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ . By expanding the linker length, FDM-35 had a BET surface of $1850\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . However, with further increase in the linker lengths, FDM-36 and -37 had the surface areas of 1265 and $1437\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The pore volumes of
FDM-34–37 followed the same trend and they were measured to be 0.475, 0.716, 0.437, and $0.524\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Fig. 2d). The surface area and pore volume drop of FDM-36 and $^{-37}$ were probably attributed to the partial structure collapse during sample activation due to the slender nature of the linkers, and the unremoved coordinated solvents under applied activation procedures. For comparison, previously reported isostructural terbium-based MOFs’ surface areas were summarized in Table S2. For the MOFs using linkers with the same length with BPyDC [19], they were reported to have similar BET surface areas with FDM-35.
The pore size distribution analysis was performed by using the nonlocal density functional theory $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ on carbon, slit pore, NLDFT equilibrium model) (Fig. 2d). FDM-34 showed two kinds of micropores, with the pore diameters less than $5.6\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and around $8.2\mathring{\mathsf{A}},$ , respectively. This result was roughly in agreement with the pore sizes obtained from single crystal structure, considering the presence of the $[(\mathsf{C H}_{3})_{2}\mathsf{N H}_{2}]^{+}$ cations in the pores. Furthermore, FDM-35, which showed the largest surface area among the four MOFs, had micropores at ${\sim}6.3$ and $10.8\mathring{\mathsf{A}}.$ On the other hand, the pore distributions of FDM-36 and -37 were much broader, showing pore sizes between ${\sim}9.0\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and ${\sim}25.0\mathring\mathrm{A}.$ Overall, the pore sizes of the MOFs gradually increased along with the linker length increase.
## 3.3. Photoluminescence originated from the interface in FDM-34–37
Rare earth-based MOFs have characteristic luminescent behaviors, which were contributed from the f-f electron transition of the metal ions. Solid state fluorescent emission spectra of FDM-34–37 were obtained to investigate the luminescence properties of the four MOFs. Before the measurements, the solvent exchanged samples were thoroughly dried under dynamic vacuum. Interestingly, FDM-35 and FDM-37 showed the signature luminescence from Tb(III), while FDM-34 and FDM-36 showed almost no luminescence.
However, we did not observe the strong characteristic emissions of Tb(III) in FDM-34 and FDM-36. FDM-34 showed one extremely weak peak $(543\mathrm{nm})$ assigned to Tb(III) in the emission spectrum (Fig. 3c). Specifically, this peak was assigned to the $^5\mathrm{D}_{4}\rightarrow^{7}\mathrm{F}_{4}$ transition, and the strong peak at $402{\mathrm{nm}}$ was probably due to the NDC linker emission (Fig. S3a for the corresponding free $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{N D C}$ emission) [32]. In the case of FDM-36, no obvious Tb(III) emission was detected (Fig. 3c). Instead, the intense emission band centered at $439\mathrm{nm}$ was attributed to the linker emission, representing a blue shift from $463\mathrm{nm}$ compared to the linker only (Fig. S3c). The small energy gap between the triplet state energy of the linkers (NDC and SBDC) and the Tb(III) $^5\mathrm{D}_{4}$ excited state energy $(\sim20500\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.\$ ) was likely to be responsible for the absence of metal emission in FDM-34 and FDM-36 [33–35].
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we have succeeded in constructing four $\left\lceil\mathrm{Tb}_{6}(-$ $\boldsymbol{0}\mathrm{H})_{8}(\mathsf{C O O})_{12}]$ -based porous MOFs with fcu-a topology, with the guidance of reticular chemistry. Different interfaces between the pores and the skeleton of the FDM-34–37 were introduced by employing organic linkers with diversified edge lengths (from 17.4 to $23.8\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\mathring{\mathsf{\Omega}}$ and functionalities (naphthalene, bipyridine, stilbene, and rotatable triphenyl rings). With the same topology, the four MOFs provided an ideal platform to systematically investigate the structure-property relationship. Their low pressure $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption behaviors were evaluated systematically, and all MOFs featured high BET surface areas (with the highest value of $1850\mathrm{m}^{2}\bar{\bf g}^{-1}$ for FDM-35). The high porosity of the MOFs guarantees feasible accessibility to incoming guests, allowing further interesting interactions on the interfaces. Furthermore, with the characteristic rare earth metals, the MOFs were studied for their properties in luminescence. With the discrepancy of the energy transfer efficiency from the linkers to the terbium centers, solid state luminescent spectra of FDM-34–37 revealed significantly discriminated luminescent characters. Considering recent studies on the linker vacancy sites, unsaturated metal sites, and postsynthetic functionalization of the linkers in MOFs, luminescent MOFs of this kind guarantee potential applications in catalysis or molecule sensing.
